WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.467
N P dot where gives us vectorized conditional logic.

00:00:04.467 --> 00:00:08.967
In this example, every score greater than or equal to ninety receives the label high.

00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:12.502
Every other score receives the label standard.

00:00:12.502 --> 00:00:17.702
Click Run and compare the numeric scores with the resulting labels.

00:00:17.702 --> 00:00:24.952
The output has the same number of positions as the input array because NumPy evaluates the condition for

00:00:24.952 --> 00:00:30.397
each score. This is similar to writing an if else decision for every element,

00:00:30.397 --> 00:00:33.752
but the operation is expressed across the whole array.

00:00:33.752 --> 00:00:37.332
N P dot where can also return numeric values.

00:00:37.332 --> 00:00:43.737
For example, you could replace negative values with zero, assign a risk score based on a threshold,

00:00:43.737 --> 00:00:46.842
or create categories from numerical measurements.

00:00:46.842 --> 00:00:51.837
Later, Pandas uses similar conditional patterns for working with columns.

00:00:51.837 --> 00:00:56.777
The main question to ask is: do I want to keep only matching values,

00:00:56.777 --> 00:01:01.827
or do I want to return one value for True and another value for False?

00:01:01.827 --> 00:01:05.862
For the second situation, where is often a useful choice.
